(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-diuretics-thiazide-quiz"]={"title":"Thiazide Diuretics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient on a thiazide develops a painful, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":["Hypercalcemia causing a stone","Hypokalemia causing weakness","Hyperuricemia causing gout","Photosensitivity causing a rash"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides cause hyperuricemia, which can lead to gout. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why can thiazides raise blood glucose?","choices":["They decrease insulin secretion","They increase insulin secretion","They increase renal glucose reabsorption","They block glucagon release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease glucose tolerance by decreasing insulin secretion, partly through increased sympathetic activity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which laboratory value is the diabetes-specific concern when a patient with diabetes starts a thiazide?","choices":["Blood glucose","Platelet count","Serum bilirubin","Serum amylase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease glucose tolerance and insulin secretion, so blood glucose can rise and should be monitored. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which statement is appropriate counseling for a patient starting hydrochlorothiazide?","choices":["Stop the drug when urine output increases","Expect blood glucose to fall","Use sun protection against photosensitivity","Expect uric acid to fall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity is a listed adverse effect, so sun protection is appropriate counseling. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which transport protein do thiazide diuretics inhibit?","choices":["The sodium-potassium-2-chloride carrier","The sodium-chloride transporter","The luminal sodium channel","The sodium-potassium pump"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The major thiazide action is inhibition of the sodium-chloride transporter on the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which history makes a thiazide reasonable because of its effect on urinary calcium?","choices":["Acute pulmonary edema","Calcium oxalate kidney stones","Gout with frequent flares","Infection-related struvite stones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By lowering urinary calcium, thiazides leave less calcium to crystallize, so they are used for calcium oxalate stones. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Why do nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs weaken the effect of thiazides?","choices":["They block the prostaglandins that aid natriuresis","They induce thiazide metabolism","They bind thiazides in the gut","They increase thiazide excretion into urine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs block prostaglandins, which attenuates the natriuretic action of thiazides. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where do thiazide diuretics act in the nephron?","choices":["Distal convoluted tubule","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides inhibit the sodium chloride transporter on the luminal membrane of the distal convoluted tubule. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"When a patient takes digoxin and a thiazide, which serum potassium level should be maintained?","choices":["Between 3.0 and 3.5 milliequivalents per liter","Above 4.0 milliequivalents per liter","Below 3.5 milliequivalents per liter","Above 6.0 milliequivalents per liter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. To limit digoxin toxicity with a thiazide, the potassium level should be kept above 4.0 milliequivalents per liter. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which pair of adverse effects can occur with thiazides?","choices":["Sexual dysfunction and gingival overgrowth","Gynecomastia and constipation","Sexual dysfunction and constipation","Hyperkalemia and diarrhea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sexual dysfunction and constipation are both adverse effects of thiazides. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which electrolyte disturbance from thiazides contributes to cardiac arrhythmia risk?","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Hyperuricemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypokalemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides increase potassium excretion, and hypokalemia, a listed adverse effect, predisposes to cardiac arrhythmias. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which acute change in renal function can thiazides cause?","choices":["An increase in glomerular filtration rate","A decrease in glomerular filtration rate","Complete loss of urine output","Permanent tubular necrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides decrease the glomerular filtration rate acutely, mostly through volume depletion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which agent is a thiazide-type diuretic?","choices":["Bumetanide","Eplerenone","Methazolamide","Chlorthalidone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chlorthalidone (Hygroton) is a thiazide-type diuretic that acts in the distal convoluted tubule. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which patient is most likely to respond well to a thiazide for hypertension?","choices":["A young patient with high renin activity","A patient with marked volume depletion","An elderly patient who retains sodium","A patient who is not retaining sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thiazides work best in the elderly, African American patients and sodium-retentive states, where removing sodium has the largest effect. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which mechanism accounts for the chronic blood pressure effect of thiazides?","choices":["Persistently lower blood volume","Persistently lower cardiac output","Higher vascular responsiveness to norepinephrine","Lower total peripheral resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronically thiazides have direct vasorelaxant effects that lower total peripheral resistance, while volume and cardiac output return toward baseline. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which of these drugs belongs with the thiazide diuretics?","choices":["Torsemide","Triamterene","Dichlorphenamide","Indapamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Indapamide (Lozol) belongs with the thiazide diuretics and blocks sodium chloride reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 20"}]};
